config BR2_PACKAGE_STEAM
	bool "steam"
	depends on BR2_x86_64 || BR2_aarch64
	depends on BR2_TOOLCHAIN_USES_GLIBC
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BASH # /usr/bin/steam is a bash script
	select BR2_PACKAGE_XWAYLAND # Steam's UI is X11
	# Every Steam binary is x86: the 32-bit bootstrap needs box86 (built
	# in the armv7 companion, merged by post-build-lib32.sh), the client
	# and games need box64.
	select BR2_PACKAGE_BOX64 if BR2_aarch64
	help
	  Valve's Steam client bootstrap.

	  This installs only the launcher (steam-launcher .deb). The Steam
	  client itself, and the Steam Runtime that provides most of its
	  libraries, are downloaded into the user's home directory on first
	  launch, so Steam needs network access the first time it runs.

	  There is no native ARM64 Steam: the launcher's own
	  detect_platform() hard-codes "ubuntu12_32" ("the only platform
	  we have a bootstrap for"), the .deb is published for amd64
	  only, and the bootstrap it unpacks is a 32-bit i386 binary.

	  So on x86_64 the client runs natively, and on ARM64 every part
	  of it is translated: the 32-bit bootstrap through box86, the
	  64-bit client and games through box64, dispatched by the
	  binfmt_misc handlers S07binfmt registers. box86 is built in the
	  armv7 companion, so an ARM64 image needs that companion merged
	  (the "lib32" profile / SWIFT_LINUX_LIB32_ROOT) or Steam cannot
	  start at all. x86_64 images need the i386 companion for the same
	  bootstrap. Both also need a running X11/XWayland session.

	  Steam is proprietary; it is not redistributed in the image sources.

	  https://store.steampowered.com/about/

comment "steam needs a glibc toolchain"
	depends on BR2_x86_64 || BR2_aarch64
	depends on !BR2_TOOLCHAIN_USES_GLIBC
